<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/ecology-populations-communities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Ecology is biology with the setting included: how living things interact with each other
    and with their surroundings. Strip away the variety of species and four questions remain,
    and they are the syllabus's four topics. Where does the <strong>energy</strong> that runs
    an ecosystem come from, and where does it go? Who eats whom — the <strong>food chains
    and webs</strong> that map feeding relationships? How do <strong>nutrients</strong> come
    back after being locked into bodies? And what makes any one <strong>population</strong>
    grow, crash or hover? Ecology's basic unit is the population — a group of the same
    species in one place — and its growth obeys a simple tension: life multiplies
    exponentially, but resources don't. The <strong>carrying capacity</strong> of the
    environment bends every real growth curve toward an S-shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    The subject rewards one habit above all: following <em>quantities</em> rather than names.
    Energy and matter move through ecosystems in trackable flows — sunlight becomes grass
    becomes wildebeest becomes lion becomes soil becomes grass again — and the arithmetic of
    those flows explains why big fierce animals are rare, why rainforests can't be farmed for
    long, and why nothing in an ecosystem is ever thrown away.
  </p>

  <h2>Energy flow: the one-way street</h2>
  <p>
    Energy enters the living world almost entirely through <strong>producers</strong> —
    plants, algae and some bacteria that photosynthesise, converting sunlight into the
    chemical energy of sugar. Everything else — <strong>consumers</strong> — eats that
    stored energy second-hand: herbivores (primary consumers) eat producers, carnivores
    (secondary consumers) eat herbivores, and so on. At every step, energy is lost in forms
    life cannot re-eat: respiration's heat (life cannot harvest heat back into work),
    unabsorbed parts, egested waste. The practical rule of thumb: <strong>only about 10% of
    the energy at one trophic level makes it to the next</strong> (measured transfers run
    roughly 5–20%). The consequence is arithmetic, not metaphor: a field carrying 1,000 kg
    of grass can support ~100 kg of herbivore and ~10 kg of carnivore. This is why food
    chains rarely exceed four or five links (there is simply nothing left to eat the
    lion-eater), why top predators are always rare and always vulnerable to extinction
    (they need the most territory per individual), and why eating lower on the food chain
    supports more people per hectare of planet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Completing the circuit are the <strong>decomposers</strong> — bacteria and fungi —
    which break down the dead and the dung and, crucially, do not <em>create</em> a trophic
    level of their own: they release locked-up nutrients for the producers to re-use. Every
    ecosystem is thus two networks in one: energy flowing one way from sun to heat, and
    matter cycling endlessly.
  </p>

  <h2>Food chains and food webs</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>food chain</strong> is one feeding pathway written linearly — grass →
    wildebeest → lion, with arrows pointing the way the energy goes. Real ecosystems are
    too retangled for chains, so ecologists draw <strong>food webs</strong>: every species
    linked to every other it eats or is eaten by. Webs show three things chains cannot.
    <strong>Redundancy:</strong> most predators eat several prey, so one collapse rarely
    starves a specialist — until you remove something connected enough. <strong>Trophic
    position is a role, not a species:</strong> a fox eats berries and rabbits, occupying
    two levels at once; humans at a banquet span three. <strong>Hierarchy can be
    quantified:</strong> ecological pyramids stack the levels as bars — a pyramid of
    <em>numbers</em> (count the organisms), of <em>biomass</em> (weigh the living tissue)
    and, the only honest one, of <em>energy</em>. Numbers and biomass pyramids occasionally
    invert (one oak tree, thousands of aphids) but an energy pyramid never does — you
    cannot support more kilojoules of consumer than the producer level makes, any more
    than a salary can be spent twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "plants are just the background; the real action is
    animals." The reverse is true energetically: producers are the <em>only</em> entry
    point of energy, typically ~1–2% of incident sunlight is captured, and every animal
    calorie is second-hand plant. Ecosystems are plant-driven from the bottom up.
  </p>

  <h2>Nutrient cycles: matter's round trips</h2>
  <p>
    Where energy is a one-way river, matter is a closed loop — the same carbon and nitrogen
    atoms have cycled through living things for billions of years. In the <strong>carbon
    cycle</strong>, photosynthesis pulls CO₂ from the air into sugar; respiration by
    producers, consumers and decomposers pushes it back; combustion of fossil fuels
    releases carbon buried for hundreds of millions of years, which is why the atmosphere's
    CO₂ has climbed from ~280 ppm (pre-industrial) to over 420 ppm today — the carbon cycle
    now runs with an extra faucet open. In the <strong>water cycle</strong> (the earth
    science lesson covers it in full), evaporation, transpiration, condensation and
    precipitation move the same water endlessly. The <strong>nitrogen cycle</strong> is the
    one with the most biology in it: air is 78% N₂, but that triple-bonded molecule is
    unusable until <em>nitrogen-fixing bacteria</em> (free-living, or housed in legume root
    nodules) convert it to ammonium and nitrates that plants can absorb; other bacteria
    return it to N₂. Farming bends this cycle heavily — the Haber process now fixes more
    nitrogen industrially than all natural land processes combined, and the excess runs
    off as the algal-bloom dead zones downstream of fertilised fields. The general lesson
    of all nutrient cycles: <strong>decomposers are not an afterthought but the return
    leg</strong> — an ecosystem that cannot recycle is a mine, not a home.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="exponential promise, logistic reality — the S-curve is ecology's first law" widget="plotter" config={ecoCfg} />

  <h2>Populations: growth, limits and cycles</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>population</strong> grows, ideally, exponentially: every individual adds
    offspring, so growth is proportional to the number already there — the red curve above,
    which given unlimited resources is what any population does (a bacterium doubling every
    20 minutes would weigh as much as the Earth in two days). Real environments bite back:
    food, space, light, water, predation, disease — the <strong>limiting factors</strong> —
    so growth slows and levels at the <strong>carrying capacity</strong> (K), the green
    logistic S-curve. The interesting part is the middle of the S, where growth is fastest
    — which is why fisheries can be harvested hardest at about half of K, and why a
    population crashing from overshoot (reindeer on St Matthew Island: 29 animals in 1944
    → over 6,000 by 1963 → tens of survivors by 1966 after the lichen ran out) is the
    lesson's cautionary worked example.
  </p>
  <p>
    Populations also <strong>couple</strong>. The classic dataset is a century of Hudson
    Bay fur records: lynx and snowshoe hare numbers rise and fall together, roughly every
    decade, with the lynx peak <em>lagging</em> the hare peak by a year or two — more hares
    feed more lynx, more lynx kill more hares, fewer hares starve the lynx, fewer lynx
    free the hares: a predator–prey oscillator built from nothing but birth and death
    rates. (Reality is messier than the textbook curve — vegetation plays a third role —
    but the lagged chase is real and reproducible in lab microcosms, as Gause showed with
    Paramecium and yeast.)
  </p>
  <p>
    Within communities, populations are organised by <strong>niches</strong> — a species'
    role and address: what it eats, when it feeds, where it shelters, what tolerances it
    has. The competitive exclusion principle (Gause, 1934) says two species cannot occupy
    the same niche indefinitely — the better competitor drives the other extinct or out —
    so coexisting species always differ <em>somehow</em>: Darwin's Galápagos finches
    partitioned beak sizes; warblers partition feeding heights in the same tree. Some nodes
    matter disproportionately: <strong>keystone species</strong> — sea otters suppressing
    sea urchins so kelp forests persist; wolves restructuring Yellowstone's rivers through
    elk behaviour (the famous trophic cascade) — whose removal rewrites the community.
    And after disturbance, communities rebuild in a predictable order,
    <strong>succession</strong>: bare rock → lichens → grasses → shrubs → woodland, each
    stage engineering the soil and shade that the next requires.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative skeleton is worth knowing. The logistic curve is
    dN/dt = rN(1 − N/K): growth is the exponential rN <em>damped</em> by the fraction of
    carrying capacity left. <strong>r and K strategists</strong> name the two ends of the
    life-history spectrum: r-selected species (insects, weeds, many fish) produce enormous
    numbers of cheap offspring to colonise fresh gaps; K-selected species (elephants,
    whales, humans) invest heavily in few offspring that compete near saturation. Most of
    conservation is deciding which regime a threatened species lives in — pandas are
    K-strategists in shrinking K, which is the whole problem. Human population history is
    the S-curve read at planetary scale: ~1 billion by 1804 after ten millennia of slow
    growth, then the demographic transition — death rates falling before birth rates —
    carried it past 8 billion by 2022, with growth now decelerating as fertility falls
    across most of the world; we are visibly on the upper bend of the S.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open edges: ecology's forecasting problem is that communities are networks with
    time lags and feedback, so linear extrapolation fails — sudden "regime shifts" (a
    lake flipping clear to turbid) are the field's version of a phase change. The
    ecosystem-services view puts the accounting on people's books: pollination (roughly a
    third of food crops depend on animal pollinators), soil formation, water purification
    — flows the nutrient and energy cycles deliver for free. And the deepest open question
    is diversity's role: <em>why</em> do more diverse communities produce more biomass and
    resist invasion more stably? Decades of experiments (the Cedar Creek grassland plots
    are the canonical case) say they do; the mechanisms — niche complementarity, sampling
    effects, insurance across species — are still being weighed. Ecology, unusually for a
    foundational unit, ends in live research.
  </p>
  <p>
    Measuring any of this requires field method, because populations are never counted
    exhaustively. Sessile or slow organisms are sampled with <strong>quadrats</strong> —
    square frames thrown (or systematically placed) many times, with the mean density
    scaled up to the habitat's area: 30 quadrats of 1 m² averaging 4 daisies means ~12
    daisies per 10 m², and the reliability comes from the number and spread of throws,
    not the frame's precision. Mobile animals use <strong>mark–recapture</strong>:
    catch, mark and release N individuals; later catch n, of which m are marked — the
    Lincoln index estimates the population as N × n ÷ m (50 marked, 40 recaptured, 10
    marked again → 200 animals), on the honest assumptions that marking neither harms
    nor changes behaviour and that the population is closed between samples. The
    assumptions <em>are</em> the lesson: every ecology number arrives with its sampling
    story attached, and reading that story is part of reading the number.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gause (1934); Odum, Fundamentals of Ecology; Krebs, Ecology; Cambridge IGCSE Biology syllabus (section 19); Ripple &amp; Beschta, Yellowstone wolf studies; Hudson's Bay fur record literature.</p>
